Facebook Module — End-to-End System Design (Stealth & Safe Outreach)
1. Objective & Scope

The Facebook module allows users to connect multiple business or personal accounts to run safe, low-risk messaging campaigns, including posts, Messenger outreach, and engagement-based signals.
The system is designed to minimize platform risk, avoid bans, and prioritize account health over speed or volume.

Key principles:

Only user-authorized accounts

Isolation of accounts and sessions

Probabilistic pacing, not rigid scheduling

Automated campaigns mimic natural human behavior

2. Core Principles

Continuity: Automation extends existing activity patterns, never “resets” behavior.

Isolation: Every Facebook account operates inside its own capsule with independent sessions, proxies, fingerprints, and activity logs.

Risk-weighted execution: Accounts with higher risk (new accounts, low activity, previous warnings) have restricted campaigns.

Fail-safe design: Any friction or warning immediately pauses the account instead of forcing execution.

AI advisory only: AI never controls timing, volume, or platform navigation; only content variation, tone, and language.

3. System Components
3.1 Account Capsule

Each connected FB account is a self-contained execution unit:

Dedicated persistent session

Fixed residential proxy (sticky IP)

Unique browser fingerprint (OS, screen size, fonts, timezone, WebGL spoofing)

Historical behavior profile (activity patterns, campaigns, responses)

Risk score (rolling, decaying with activity)

No state or sessions are shared between accounts.

3.2 Campaign Engine

Campaigns operate as intent pipelines:

Entry methods: Messenger, post comment, story response

Message phases: initial + follow-ups

Eligibility rules: account activity, friend/follower connections, prior engagement

Exit conditions: reply received, rejection, anomaly detected

Execution is event-driven, not volume-driven.

3.3 Message Strategy

First outreach is context-light and non-transactional

Links or heavy sales content are avoided in initial messages

Follow-ups decrease in assertiveness and introduce new value

Templates act as semantic frameworks, never rigid text

AI may generate variations, tone adjustments, or minor phrasing edits

4. Execution Model
4.1 Temporal & Pacing Control

Activity windows are probabilistic (start/stop time drifts daily)

Randomized delays between actions simulate human behavior

Days of inactivity are enforced naturally

4.2 Action Selection

At each opportunity, the system chooses one of three:

Act — send Messenger, comment, or react

Observe — scroll news feed or view stories without interaction

Idle — do nothing

Non-action is treated as legitimate activity.

4.3 Entry Methods

Messenger DM: Only to friends or previously interacted users; avoids unsolicited mass messaging

Story interactions / reactions: Used to warm targets before messaging

Post engagement: Optional, low-volume likes/comments to maintain normal footprint

The system avoids chaining multiple entry methods on a single target in short succession.

5. Volume & Cap Management

Hard caps are dynamic, based on account age, prior activity, and recent friction:

Messenger messages: 20–50/day

Story interactions: 30–60/day

Post reactions/comments: 20–50/day

Soft caps: system aims below hard limits, adjusts upward only with safe history

Randomization prevents pattern recognition (time, count, interval)

6. Content & Messaging Safety

Avoid spammy or trigger words (“ROI,” “automation,” “leads”)

First messages are link-free

Template variation: tone, sentence length, punctuation, casual human-like style

Imperfect grammar or occasional lowercase accepted

Follow-ups must be delayed and varied in tone

7. Follow-up Logic

Delay between follow-ups: ≥24 hours

Content variation required for every follow-up

Thread stops immediately on reply (positive or negative)

No repeated messaging after response

8. Detection & Self-Protection

Early warning signals: captcha, login prompts, send failures, message restrictions

Immediate pause: 48–72h cooldown if triggered

Cooldown adjustment: subsequent campaign volume reduced, execution slowed

Account isolation: no other accounts use same proxy/session until cooldown complete

9. AI Agent Usage

AI may assist with:

Message variation

Tone matching

Rewriting templates

AI may NOT:

Control execution timing

Decide campaign volume

Override safety caps

10. Failure Containment

Capsule-level failure prevents any cross-account contamination

No shared proxies or fingerprints across accounts

Campaigns continue only if all safety checks pass

11. Explicitly Avoided Behaviors

Mass scraping or friend collection

Fixed, repetitive numeric limits

Forced aggressive outreach

Multi-account chaining under same proxy

Ignoring platform friction signals

Hard-coded sequences of messages or reactions

12. Outcome

Campaigns advance slowly and safely

Accounts remain long-lived

User experience mimics human-like platform use, avoiding bans

System prioritizes health and stealth over aggressive growth